1
00:00:08,940 --> 00:00:17,250
Hi, good morning. It's wonderful to be back here. Wonderful to see you here.

2
00:00:19,550 --> 00:00:29,550
I'm teaching this class because I wish a class like this had been taught when I was sitting in your seats as an undergrad here.

3
00:00:30,530 --> 00:00:42,050
This does not mean that it's a class you wish to be taught, nor does it mean that it's the right class for you. What I hope to do in the next couple of lectures is give you an idea of what this class is about

4
00:00:42,050 --> 00:00:46,590
so that you can decide whether or not it is for you.

5
00:00:48,940 --> 00:00:58,110
I came here in 1992 and started off as a computer science concentrator

6
00:00:58,970 --> 00:01:03,130
and then had a mini epiphany halfway through my sophomore year.

7
00:01:05,240 --> 00:01:14,100
I realized that I was in a wonderful place with wonderful students around me, wonderful teachers.

8
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:21,420
I was doing well academically, I was doing well in athletics, I was playing varsity squash

9
00:01:21,420 --> 00:01:29,040
at the time, I was doing well socially, everything was going well, except for the fact that

10
00:01:29,040 --> 00:01:33,740
I was unhappy, and I didn't understand why.

11
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:42,140
And it was then, in a matter of moments, that I decided that I had to find out why and become

12
00:01:42,140 --> 00:01:50,260
happier. And that's when I switched my concentration from computer science to philosophy and psychology

13
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:59,930
with a single question, how can I become happier? Over time, I did become happier. What had

14
00:01:59,930 --> 00:02:10,590
contributed most to my happiness was when I encountered a new emerging field. At that time, it didn't have the name that it has today, but essentially research that falls

15
00:02:10,590 --> 00:02:14,470
under or within the field of positive psychology.

16
00:02:17,230 --> 00:02:25,130
Positive psychology, studying it, applying the ideas to my life has made me significantly happier. It continues to make me happier.

17
00:02:25,810 --> 00:02:36,970
And it was when I realized the impact that it had on me that I decided to share it with others. That's when I decided that I wanted to be a teacher and teach this field.

18
00:02:40,600 --> 00:02:44,760
So this is positive psychology, psychology 1504.

19
00:02:45,840 --> 00:02:50,340
And we'll be exploring this new, relatively new, and fascinating field.

20
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:55,520
And hopefully, we will be exploring more than the field ourselves.

21
00:02:57,260 --> 00:03:04,400
When I first taught this class, it was back in 2002, I taught it as a seminar and had eight students.

22
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:10,960
Two dropped out. That left me with six.

23
00:03:12,020 --> 00:03:19,420
The year after, the class became slightly larger. I had over 300 students.

24
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:24,400
And then the third year when I taught it, which was the last time,

25
00:03:25,120 --> 00:03:33,160
I had 850 students in the class, making it at that point the largest course at Harvard.

26
00:03:33,740 --> 00:03:44,800
And that's when the media became interested, because they wanted to understand why. They wanted to understand this phenomenon that here you have a class that's larger than introduction to economics.

27
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:47,300
How could that be?

28
00:03:47,660 --> 00:03:53,920
So I was invited by the media for interviews, whether it was newspapers, radio, television.

29
00:03:55,700 --> 00:03:59,240
And I started to notice a pattern during those interviews.

30
00:03:59,780 --> 00:04:11,140
So I would walk into the interview, we'd have the interview, and afterwards, the producer or the interviewer would walk me out and say something to the effect of, well, thank you, Tal, for the interview,

31
00:04:11,820 --> 00:04:15,220
but, you know, I expected you to be different.

32
00:04:17,040 --> 00:04:22,600
And I would ask as nonchalantly as I could, of course, as if I didn't really care, but I had to ask anyway,

33
00:04:24,240 --> 00:04:25,040
how different?

34
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:30,020
And they would say, well, you know, we expected you to be more...

35
00:04:31,240 --> 00:04:35,620
next interview at the end of the interview same thing thank you for doing the interview but you know Tal

36
00:04:36,340 --> 00:04:46,080
I expected you to be different and once again nonchalantly of course I would ask so how different and here she would say

37
00:04:46,760 --> 00:04:47,680
well you know

38
00:04:48,380 --> 00:04:51,340
we expected you to be less less introverted

39
00:04:53,780 --> 00:04:54,880
next interview

40
00:04:55,680 --> 00:05:01,140
same thing how different well you know more extroverted more outgoing

41
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:02,960
next interview

42
00:05:02,960 --> 00:05:19,460
Well, you know, less shy, because, you know, I get very nervous in interviews, interview after interview, literally dozens, more outgoing, more cheerful, less introverted, more extroverted, and on and on.

43
00:05:19,800 --> 00:05:21,220
But here is the best one.

44
00:05:21,860 --> 00:05:27,160
So this is one of the local channels here in around Boston.

45
00:05:27,600 --> 00:05:32,020
I go into the interview. We have quite a long interview, which I thought was actually pretty good.

46
00:05:33,140 --> 00:05:42,040
And at the end of the interview, the interviewer, who's a very jolly guy, walks me out, puts his hand on my shoulder and says, Tal, thank you very much for doing the interview.

47
00:05:43,200 --> 00:05:47,480
And then the usual comes. But you know, Tal, I expected you to be different.

48
00:05:49,200 --> 00:05:56,350
And I said, how different? And just so that you understand, by this time, my self-esteem is shot.

49
00:05:56,650 --> 00:06:01,250
But still, but still, with some semblance of nonchalance, ask, how different?

50
00:06:02,670 --> 00:06:09,670
And he looks at me and says, well, I don't know, Tal, I expected you to be taller.

51
00:06:13,640 --> 00:06:19,560
Taller, what, 5'7", or okay, 5'6 1⁄2", is not enough to teach happiness.

52
00:06:22,260 --> 00:06:26,240
And I thought about it, and I thought about it a lot, the whole pattern from the beginning.

53
00:06:27,380 --> 00:06:37,160
And I think I understand why they expected someone different. You see, they had to explain to themselves, as well as the audience, How come this class is larger than Introduction to Economics?

54
00:06:38,250 --> 00:06:45,620
And the way to explain it must be that the teacher is very outgoing,

55
00:06:45,920 --> 00:06:51,220
extremely charismatic, very cheerful, an extrovert, and, of course, tall.

56
00:06:53,570 --> 00:07:04,640
Well, there was one L missing there, but, yeah, if only.

57
00:07:06,720 --> 00:07:12,500
So, the problem, though, is that they were looking in the wrong place for the explanation.

58
00:07:12,900 --> 00:07:16,180
In other words, they were looking at the messenger.

59
00:07:16,540 --> 00:07:19,980
Where they needed to look at was at the message.

60
00:07:20,540 --> 00:07:29,380
Now, how do I know that? You see, because I see other positive psychology classes on other campuses around the country and around the world.

61
00:07:29,500 --> 00:07:40,700
There are over 200 campuses here in the United States that teach positive psychology. On almost every campus where this class is taught, it's either one of the or the largest class.

62
00:07:41,240 --> 00:07:52,920
It's about the message. I see more and more organizations taking up positive psychology in their, as consultant companies, some of the leading, the big consulting companies are taking it on.

63
00:07:53,240 --> 00:07:58,980
More and more high schools are introducing positive psychology classes. Elementary schools are introducing it.

64
00:07:59,560 --> 00:08:04,240
Governments around the world are expressing an interest in this new and emerging field.

65
00:08:08,140 --> 00:08:08,660
Why?

66
00:08:09,580 --> 00:08:16,380
Because it works. Because it really works. You see, this whole realm of life flourishing, of happiness, of well-being,

67
00:08:16,920 --> 00:08:26,700
has been, until recently, dominated by the self-help movement. What do we have in the self-help movement? We have books that are very interesting, that are very accessible.

68
00:08:26,700 --> 00:08:32,640
We have speakers who are very outgoing, very charismatic and tall,

69
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:38,080
attracting in the masses into these workshops and seminars and lectures.

70
00:08:39,120 --> 00:08:47,960
But, and there is a very big but here, many of these books, many of these workshops and seminars lack substance,

71
00:08:48,480 --> 00:08:55,640
very often over-promising and under-delivering. So these are the five things you need to know to be happy.

72
00:08:56,180 --> 00:08:58,780
The three things to be the great leader.

73
00:08:59,960 --> 00:09:06,020
The one secret of success, happiness, and the perfect love life.

74
00:09:06,680 --> 00:09:09,560
Over-promising, under-delivering.

75
00:09:10,740 --> 00:09:20,880
On the other hand, we have academia. What do we have in academia? We have a lot of rigor, a lot of substance. We have data that's analyzed, re-analyzed, and meta-analyzed.

76
00:09:21,320 --> 00:09:23,240
Things that actually work.

77
00:09:23,800 --> 00:09:24,860
Good stuff.

78
00:09:25,640 --> 00:09:29,080
But, and there is also a very big but here,

79
00:09:30,480 --> 00:09:35,860
very few people read, refereed academic journals.

80
00:09:36,360 --> 00:09:39,100
I mean, think about it. How many people outside of this room, of course,

81
00:09:39,880 --> 00:09:44,360
have read the last 12 issues of the Journal of Personality and Social Psychology?

82
00:09:46,320 --> 00:09:48,220
Most people don't even know what that means.

83
00:09:49,920 --> 00:09:57,480
The head of my PhD program actually estimated that the average academic journal article is read by seven people.

84
00:10:00,570 --> 00:10:07,360
You know, and that includes the author's mother.

85
00:10:07,920 --> 00:10:17,940
So, you know, and I say it half in jest, but it's actually really sad because, certainly sad for me as an academic, because these things are good, they're important,

86
00:10:17,940 --> 00:10:21,080
things that make a difference can make even more of a difference,

87
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:30,560
but not accessible to most people. And this is where positive psychology comes in. And this is also where this class comes in.

88
00:10:30,780 --> 00:10:39,320
The explicit mandate of positive psychology, as well as of this class, is to create a bridge between the ivory tower and main street.

89
00:10:40,360 --> 00:10:45,060
In other words, it is to bring the rigor, the substance, the empirical foundation,

90
00:10:46,380 --> 00:10:56,060
the science from academia and merge it with the accessibility of the self-help or new age movement.

91
00:10:57,120 --> 00:11:04,540
In a way, the best of both worlds. And this explains the popularity of the field of positive psychology.

92
00:11:05,600 --> 00:11:07,200
Science that works.

93
00:11:10,680 --> 00:11:13,320
This class will be taught on two levels.

94
00:11:13,740 --> 00:11:20,040
The first level, it will be taught like any other class in psychology or any of the classes that you have taken here.

95
00:11:20,800 --> 00:11:27,200
You'll be introduced here to studies, to research, to rigorous academic work.

96
00:11:27,840 --> 00:11:31,360
You will be writing a paper, an academic paper.

97
00:11:32,040 --> 00:11:35,080
You'll be taking exams, just like every other class.

98
00:11:36,690 --> 00:11:39,330
But then it will also be taught at a second level,

99
00:11:40,110 --> 00:11:51,270
which is for every paper that you'll read, every paper that you will write, you will always be thinking, okay, so how can I take these ideas and apply them to my life?

100
00:11:51,490 --> 00:11:55,210
How can I apply them to my relationship? How can I apply them to my community?

101
00:11:56,610 --> 00:11:57,570
Two levels.

102
00:11:58,090 --> 00:12:00,070
The academic applied.

103
00:12:01,670 --> 00:12:10,510
I did not just introduce, whether it's in the readings or in lectures, ideas, just because they're interesting for the sake of the idea.

104
00:12:11,070 --> 00:12:15,330
It's always an idea that is both rigorous and can be applied.

105
00:12:24,240 --> 00:12:26,800
Just a few words about housekeeping,

106
00:12:27,260 --> 00:12:31,160
some of the questions that I've already received from you before the class started.

107
00:12:31,720 --> 00:12:39,240
So this, unfortunately, is the last time that I'm teaching positive psychology or any other class, for that matter, at Harvard.

108
00:12:41,600 --> 00:12:50,560
Hopefully, within two years, probably not next year, but within two years, there will be positive psychology class offered, but I certainly cannot guarantee it.

109
00:12:55,190 --> 00:12:57,350
About feedback and questions.

110
00:12:58,590 --> 00:13:07,490
If you have any questions, if anything is not clear, If you agree or disagree with something, email me or email your TF and we'll always respond.

111
00:13:07,710 --> 00:13:17,830
Sometimes if the question is asked by enough people, we'll respond to it publicly, always anonymously, unless you specify specifically that your name can be mentioned.

112
00:13:21,110 --> 00:13:28,050
But sometimes you may be listening to a lecture and then halfway through it, there's an emergency.

113
00:13:29,090 --> 00:13:34,190
there is something that you really have to ask something that cannot wait

114
00:13:35,370 --> 00:13:36,570
in that case

115
00:13:37,350 --> 00:13:42,210
please just put your hand out because you know it's like when you just have to go to the bathroom

116
00:13:43,310 --> 00:13:45,370
just can't stop, can't wait

117
00:13:46,170 --> 00:13:56,250
and when you gotta go you gotta go so we'll take a positive psychology break for that

118
00:13:57,110 --> 00:14:01,990
and just stop me halfway through and I'll answer any question.

119
00:14:06,310 --> 00:14:10,830
All the PowerPoints, as well as the videos of the classes, will be online,

120
00:14:11,310 --> 00:14:21,010
will be available within a couple of days. Well, the PowerPoints will be available before, except for this lecture will be available before, so that you can use them in class.

121
00:14:21,790 --> 00:14:31,130
The videos, unfortunately, cannot be made available before. We tried, couldn't figure it out. So they will be available within a day or two after.

122
00:14:33,330 --> 00:14:38,490
And the reason why they're up there, first of all, I do prefer that you attend lecture.

123
00:14:39,170 --> 00:14:41,650
I do prefer that you're physically here.

124
00:14:43,490 --> 00:14:54,070
You get things in the energy of the room with so many students that you wouldn't get just in front of your computer. But the reason why I do put them up is so that you have an opportunity if you want to see it again

125
00:14:54,070 --> 00:14:57,450
or if you happen to miss a class, that's perfectly fine.

126
00:14:59,290 --> 00:15:07,510
And also, because, and this is also the reason why the PowerPoints are always available, I want you to be engaged in the material.

127
00:15:07,950 --> 00:15:17,450
I want you to be engaged in whatever it is that we're discussing in class, not necessarily thinking about getting down every word that I say on paper,

128
00:15:17,810 --> 00:15:29,270
remembering everything, memorizing everything. I want you to take rather than passive notes of writing down what it says on the PowerPoint or every word that I say, I'd like you to take active notes.

129
00:15:29,690 --> 00:15:39,690
And that means being engaged with the material. For example, if you hear something, an idea that you say, oh, that's interesting, you know, star it, write it down.

130
00:15:40,050 --> 00:15:42,470
Or, okay, I think I'm going to start applying this.

131
00:15:42,950 --> 00:15:43,810
Write it down.

132
00:15:44,750 --> 00:15:51,050
Or I want to tell my mom about this later. Or I want to talk to my roommates or my team about this idea.

133
00:15:51,830 --> 00:15:52,670
Write it down.

134
00:15:53,250 --> 00:16:00,830
Active note-taking as opposed to passive note-taking. for two reasons. First of all, as I said, this class is a class about making a difference in

135
00:16:00,830 --> 00:16:11,570
people's lives. I would not be teaching the class just for its academic beauty, though there is a lot of academic beauty in this field. So write down if you have ideas that you think you can apply.

136
00:16:12,170 --> 00:16:23,410
The second reason why we should do that is because you'll remember more, better retention, better understanding of the material if you're actively engaged, as opposed to just taking

137
00:16:23,410 --> 00:16:37,180
down passive notes. Throughout the class, starting next week, we'll take what I call

138
00:16:37,180 --> 00:16:44,660
time ins as opposed to time outs. It's like a time out. It's a time where we stop the class

139
00:16:44,660 --> 00:16:50,680
and you look inward. And this is literally a time of silence in the class. I will stop

140
00:16:50,680 --> 00:16:57,960
for a minute or two and you'll have a chance either to just stare at me or anyone else or

141
00:16:58,500 --> 00:17:02,520
think about what we've just discussed or have a guiding question that I will

142
00:17:03,080 --> 00:17:14,520
provide you that you will address during the class. The reason why I have time is, and this is something that I'm introducing this year for the first time, we didn't have it last time, is because over the last two years

143
00:17:14,520 --> 00:17:18,560
since I last started, I've done a lot of work in the area of silence.

144
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:22,020
I've read a lot of research in this area

145
00:17:22,700 --> 00:17:30,920
about the importance of quiet times. Whether it's in a class, in a lecture, whether it is at home, whether it's for a leader in a business,

146
00:17:31,460 --> 00:17:35,740
for a relationship, for children, starting from preschools.

147
00:17:36,880 --> 00:17:42,480
Now, many of you, as you're going through these timings, if you decide to take the class,

148
00:17:43,560 --> 00:17:54,000
may think, well, you know, is this what I'm paying $40,000 a year for, to sit in a class and be quiet? Well, first of all, it will only be a minute or two at a time, maybe once or twice a lecture.

149
00:17:54,420 --> 00:17:58,440
But second, it may be the most important thing that you take from this class,

150
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,680
the notion of embracing stillness.

151
00:18:02,440 --> 00:18:13,260
Let me read to you an excerpt from a study that was run by two MIT professors. By the way, all the names that I read that are not on the PowerPoints,

152
00:18:13,320 --> 00:18:17,120
you don't need to remember or write down. This is just for your edification.

153
00:18:19,160 --> 00:18:21,140
So David Foster and Matthew Wilson.

154
00:18:22,160 --> 00:18:31,640
Both of them are from MIT and did the following study that I think confirms the importance of a time in, time to look inside.

155
00:18:31,940 --> 00:18:42,760
What they did was they scanned the brains of rats while they were in a maze and after they went through the maze.

156
00:18:42,900 --> 00:18:44,000
And here is what they found.

157
00:18:46,080 --> 00:18:56,260
What the results suggest is that while there certainly is some record of your experience as it is occurring, in other words, when they were doing the maze.

158
00:18:56,660 --> 00:19:06,100
The actual learning, when you try to figure out what was important, what should I keep and throw away, that happens after the fact,

159
00:19:06,500 --> 00:19:14,740
during periods of quiet, wakeful introspection. What they showed was rats who went through the maze and then went through the maze again and again,

160
00:19:15,040 --> 00:19:20,160
learned far less than rats who took time aside,

161
00:19:20,160 --> 00:19:27,920
chilled out a little bit after a maze, had a margarita, experienced, embraced stillness.

162
00:19:28,480 --> 00:19:36,440
This has implications, and they showed implications to human beings as well, not only those who are on the rat race, all human beings.

163
00:19:36,660 --> 00:19:47,120
So what they say is that replay might constitute a general mechanism of learning and memory, both learning, understanding, as well as memory, retention.

164
00:19:47,120 --> 00:19:58,260
When we reflect, when we replay the material, we're much more likely to retain, to remember what we have just been through.

165
00:19:59,040 --> 00:20:04,400
So the importance of time aside cannot be overemphasized.

166
00:20:05,340 --> 00:20:14,940
In his wonderful book on teaching, Parker Palmer, it's called The Courage to Teach, says the following.

167
00:20:16,100 --> 00:20:25,800
Words are not the sole medium of exchange in teaching and learning We educate with silence as well Silence gives us a chance to reflect on what we have said and heard

168
00:20:25,800 --> 00:20:36,880
In authentic education, silence is treated as a trustworthy metrics for the inner work students must do A medium for learning of the deepest sort

169
00:20:38,220 --> 00:20:44,920
Silence is something that's missing from our culture I know that many of you have probably read Zen and the Art of Motorcycle

170
00:20:44,940 --> 00:20:50,880
maintenance, Robert M. Persig. Well, he has a second book out, less well-known, called

171
00:20:50,880 --> 00:20:57,860
Lila. And Lila is an anthropological study of Native Americans. And what he does there

172
00:20:57,860 --> 00:21:06,700
is compare their culture to Americans from European ancestry. And one of the distinguishing

173
00:21:06,700 --> 00:21:15,960
characteristics between these two cultures is their approach towards silence. What he found was

174
00:21:15,960 --> 00:21:23,500
when he went and sat around with Native Americans, they would sit around the fire and hang out for

175
00:21:23,500 --> 00:21:32,440
two, three hours without saying a word. Just sit around, look at one another, smile, have a good

176
00:21:32,440 --> 00:21:38,200
time introspect just be there for hours whereas he points out that in our culture we feel very

177
00:21:38,200 --> 00:21:44,880
uncomfortable with the absence of words with the absence of sound or noise we have to fill up all

178
00:21:44,880 --> 00:21:50,520
the gaps and this is an important cultural difference and we pay a price for this lack

179
00:21:50,520 --> 00:22:04,720
of stillness a price that i will talk about a lot when i talk about relationships when i talk about

180
00:22:04,720 --> 00:22:09,920
virtue and morality, and when we talk about happiness and well-being in general.

181
00:22:17,200 --> 00:22:27,520
Let me give you a little bit of background on positive psychology, how it came about and how this class came about. In many ways, positive psychology is the brainchild,

182
00:22:27,740 --> 00:22:33,480
the product, the grandchild of humanistic psychology. What we have in humanistic psychology

183
00:22:33,480 --> 00:22:41,140
is essentially a reaction to the existing psychologies of the time.

184
00:22:42,280 --> 00:22:45,800
The founders, considered the founders of humanistic psychology,

185
00:22:46,180 --> 00:22:57,120
in fact called it the third force. Why the third force? Because the first force was behaviorism, the work of Skinner, the work of Watson, the work of Thorndike.

186
00:22:57,940 --> 00:23:08,960
This was the first force. The second force was psychoanalysis, the work of Freud, Jung, Adler to some extent.

187
00:23:09,360 --> 00:23:15,760
This was the second force. And the third force, humanistic psychology, came as a reaction to it.

188
00:23:16,380 --> 00:23:18,780
First is a reaction to behaviorism.

189
00:23:19,360 --> 00:23:26,180
Behaviorism looks at the human entity, at the person, as basically a collection of behaviors,

190
00:23:26,180 --> 00:23:35,320
as a box, like a billiard ball knocked around by reinforcements, by punishment, by reward.

191
00:23:38,580 --> 00:23:44,460
And what humanistic psychology said was that we're much more than just a billiard ball being knocked around.

192
00:23:46,380 --> 00:23:51,820
We have a spirit, we have a soul, we have cognitions and thoughts that matter.

193
00:23:52,120 --> 00:23:58,580
It's not just behavior that's important for understanding as well as improving life.

194
00:23:58,580 --> 00:24:04,710
And then psychoanalysis, the second force.

195
00:24:04,850 --> 00:24:15,030
What psychoanalysis is about, basically understanding mostly through the subconscious. That's how you understand it. That's how you improve the quality of life.

196
00:24:16,170 --> 00:24:27,090
There are defense mechanisms. There are biological instincts, neuroses. And if we understand these, very often dark forces were better able to handle and deal with life,

197
00:24:27,090 --> 00:24:30,210
understand as well as improve the quality of life.

198
00:24:33,080 --> 00:24:39,900
Humanistic psychology says a human being is much more than that, much more than biological instincts, much more than neurosis,

199
00:24:40,860 --> 00:24:47,380
much more than a person who exists in a Newtonian reality like a billiard ball.

200
00:24:48,360 --> 00:24:59,220
We need to value much more the human being. We need to give much more dignity and freedom to the person.

201
00:25:01,240 --> 00:25:07,440
There was a problem. The problem was with humanistic psychology is that it lacked the rigorous methodology.

202
00:25:08,540 --> 00:25:17,620
While it brought in many wonderful ideas, talked about the study of well-being, talked about the study of optimism, of kindness, of morality, of virtue, of love,

203
00:25:17,740 --> 00:25:27,560
relationships, of peak experiences, of self-actualization, of empathy, all these wonderful concepts that we'll talk about throughout the semester,

204
00:25:27,560 --> 00:25:34,760
it wasn't as rigorous about its epistemology, about how we form ideas and how we learn.

205
00:25:35,120 --> 00:25:41,240
And that's why in many ways, largely, not completely, but it largely morphed into the self-help movement.

206
00:25:42,240 --> 00:25:44,480
Interesting ideas, good ideas, important ideas,

207
00:25:44,840 --> 00:25:45,800
certainly good intentions,

208
00:25:47,040 --> 00:25:57,440
but to some extent without the academic rigor. And that's why it lacked to have the impact on academia. That's why we don't have hardly any humanistic psychology classes offered at universities today.

209
00:25:57,560 --> 00:26:03,740
There are very few still around, and this is why also a lot of it became the New Age, essentially.

210
00:26:05,180 --> 00:26:13,460
But still, it's humanistic psychology that has, in many ways, fathered and mothered, as we'll see, positive psychology.

211
00:26:13,720 --> 00:26:23,320
So let's meet the grandparents, people like Rollo May, people like Carl Rogers, and more than anyone, Abraham Maslow.

212
00:26:23,320 --> 00:26:33,960
was the American Psychological Association president, was a professor just down the road here at Brandeis, and he introduced this humanistic psychology.

213
00:26:34,200 --> 00:26:40,000
In 1954, he wrote a chapter called Toward a Positive Psychology.

214
00:26:41,020 --> 00:26:41,680
1954.

215
00:26:42,240 --> 00:26:48,520
In it, he said, we need to also research kindness and goodness and happiness and optimism.

216
00:26:49,600 --> 00:26:53,100
In many ways, he was way ahead of his time.

217
00:26:55,240 --> 00:27:03,040
then if maslow is the grandfather then karen horny is the grandmother initially a psychoanalyst

218
00:27:03,040 --> 00:27:14,920
trained through the works of freud realized that the focus has been too much on the negative on neurosis on psychosis and said we also not only but also have to focus on what is working in the

219
00:27:14,920 --> 00:27:22,980
human organism. We have to look at the fine qualities and cultivate those, because part

220
00:27:22,980 --> 00:27:30,460
of being human is being those things as well. In many ways, brought about the movement toward

221
00:27:30,460 --> 00:27:39,600
humanistic psychology and through that, positive psychology. Aaron Antonovsky, the third person

222
00:27:39,600 --> 00:27:47,300
and I would consider it the grandfather, brought in the idea of focusing on health.

223
00:27:47,880 --> 00:27:53,550
He has a new concept, or he introduced a new concept.

224
00:27:53,950 --> 00:27:55,470
I'm still here, don't worry.

225
00:27:56,690 --> 00:28:03,730
He introduced a new concept, which he called, it's his own neologism,

226
00:28:07,530 --> 00:28:08,890
from salutogenesis.

227
00:28:11,650 --> 00:28:22,130
Salutogenesis, salutohelf, genesis, origin. The origin of health. And this was an alternative model to the conventional was the model of pathologies.

228
00:28:23,130 --> 00:28:31,830
So instead of just studying pathologies, whether it's in physical health or psychological health, we should also study the origin of health.

229
00:28:32,470 --> 00:28:41,010
In many ways, that is what preventative medicine is about. So this was a novel idea back in the 1970s when he introduced it.

230
00:28:41,110 --> 00:28:44,570
And we'll talk a lot about Aaron Antonovsky.

231
00:28:46,010 --> 00:28:47,230
Now the parents.

232
00:28:49,890 --> 00:28:55,190
Marty Seligman, considered the father of the Positive Psychology Network of Scholars,

233
00:28:56,190 --> 00:29:06,810
started the field in 1998. Like Maslow, he too was the president of the American Psychological Association. and as his mandate during his presidency, he had two aims.

234
00:29:07,070 --> 00:29:17,750
The first aim, to make academic psychology more accessible. In other words, bridge Ivory Tower and Main Street. This was the first aim of his presidency.

235
00:29:17,750 --> 00:29:25,110
The second aim was to introduce a positive psychology, a psychology that will look at also things that work,

236
00:29:25,190 --> 00:29:30,690
that will not just study depression, anxiety, schizophrenia or neurosis.

237
00:29:31,550 --> 00:29:40,890
A psychology, a network of scholars who will focus on love, relationship, self-esteem, motivation, resilience, and well-being.

238
00:29:41,510 --> 00:29:48,580
And he introduced these ideas, and it's all been literally uphill from then.

239
00:29:49,320 --> 00:29:55,740
Before Marty Seligman, this happened all in 1998, and we'll talk about it a little bit more next time. This all happened in 1998.

240
00:29:55,740 --> 00:30:03,880
Long before 1998, our very own Professor Alan Langer did research in all these areas,

241
00:30:04,260 --> 00:30:09,280
bringing the humanistic spirit and combining it with the academic scientific rigor.

242
00:30:10,540 --> 00:30:20,460
We're going to be talking probably more than about any other scholar about her work in this class. And another person from Harvard, one of the parents of positive psychology,

243
00:30:20,740 --> 00:30:25,060
was Philip Stone, who passed away two years ago.

244
00:30:25,840 --> 00:30:35,020
yesterday. Both Langer and Stone were my thesis advisors, introduced me to the field of positive

245
00:30:35,020 --> 00:30:41,820
psychology, into this research. In 1998, when they had the first positive psychology summit,

246
00:30:42,440 --> 00:30:53,760
Professor Stone took me along with him. I was his graduate student. In 1999, he taught the first positive psychology class at Harvard, one of the first in the world. I was his teaching fellow.

247
00:30:54,320 --> 00:31:03,560
A couple of years later, he taught it again. Again, I was his teaching fellow. And then, when I graduated, he suggested that I take over his class.

248
00:31:04,880 --> 00:31:06,860
And here we are today.

249
00:31:11,920 --> 00:31:19,640
So this is 1504. Let me give you a sense of, in the next half hour or so, a sense of what to expect in this class.

250
00:31:20,180 --> 00:31:29,380
The first thing is that this class is not just about information. It is also explicitly about transformation.

251
00:31:30,200 --> 00:31:31,540
What do I mean by that?

252
00:31:32,200 --> 00:31:37,460
You see, most of education today is about information. What is information?

253
00:31:38,500 --> 00:31:46,740
So we have a container, which is our mind,

254
00:31:47,480 --> 00:31:57,360
and information is about taking data, taking science, taking information, and putting it inside the form. That is information.

255
00:31:57,820 --> 00:32:03,120
Now, when this form is filled, that's when we're educated.

256
00:32:05,030 --> 00:32:08,330
More information, more data, better.

257
00:32:11,880 --> 00:32:12,860
Not enough.

258
00:32:14,500 --> 00:32:25,540
Because it's not just information that determines our well-being, our success, our self-esteem, our motivational level, the relationship and the quality of our relationships.

259
00:32:27,000 --> 00:32:28,960
It's much more than information.

260
00:32:30,920 --> 00:32:40,200
Transformation is about taking this form and changing it. Trans, change, form, shape.

261
00:32:40,560 --> 00:32:41,620
Change the form.

262
00:32:42,000 --> 00:32:43,380
This is transformation.

263
00:32:43,780 --> 00:32:49,440
This is a distinction that I learned first from at school, Professor Robert Keegan, who talks about this.

264
00:32:51,760 --> 00:32:55,220
Information in and of itself is not enough. Think about this example.

265
00:32:57,380 --> 00:33:06,700
You go for an athletic meet. Your aim is to get into the top three, to be a medalist. You come in number eight.

266
00:33:10,660 --> 00:33:12,740
What's the analysis?

267
00:33:13,140 --> 00:33:19,180
What is the interpretation? Terrible. I just failed. You feel deflated, enervated.

268
00:33:20,100 --> 00:33:29,240
On the other hand, the exact same event. You came eighth when you expect to be top three. You can interpret it as, okay, so what have I learned?

269
00:33:29,640 --> 00:33:40,920
I need to work even harder. You become even more energized. you learn from the experience. In other words, the same objective information, which is, I got eighth, I expect a top three.

270
00:33:41,260 --> 00:33:45,840
The same information, very different interpretation.

271
00:33:47,620 --> 00:33:52,500
One is a disaster, the other interpretation is an opportunity.

272
00:33:52,940 --> 00:33:59,420
One leads to loss of energy, the other one to an increase in energy. Or think about another very common example.

273
00:33:59,980 --> 00:34:05,220
We know of many people around the world who seemingly have everything,

274
00:34:05,840 --> 00:34:16,230
who are doing well, who have more than they need, and yet they're unhappy. And then there are people around the world who have very little,

275
00:34:18,220 --> 00:34:29,840
and yet never cease, never stop to celebrate life. And then we have the other way around as well, people who have everything and appreciate it and enjoy life, and people who have very little who see themselves as victim.

276
00:34:32,470 --> 00:34:40,120
In other words, it's not just the information that goes in, it's also the shape, the interpretation, the perception, the focus.

277
00:34:41,420 --> 00:34:45,500
And that is determined by the shape of the form.

278
00:34:48,020 --> 00:34:50,580
This is what I realized when I was an undergrad here.

279
00:34:51,320 --> 00:34:54,400
Seemingly, looking in from the outside, had everything.

280
00:34:55,220 --> 00:35:05,960
Doing well, sports, academics, socially, and yet my perception, my focus, my interpretation of life, not that great, wasn't happy.

281
00:35:08,880 --> 00:35:16,560
The interpretation matters very often a lot more, as we'll see, than the information that goes in.

282
00:35:18,160 --> 00:35:29,500
One of the sentences that I'll repeat throughout the class is that happiness is much more contingent on our state of mind than on our status or the state of our bank account.

283
00:35:29,500 --> 00:35:37,500
and that's where transformation comes in and that's why it's so important for well-being.

284
00:35:39,760 --> 00:35:43,380
What that will look like in practice

285
00:35:44,380 --> 00:35:55,940
is that we will cover not so much information, we'll uncover much more. And I don't mean that in the Berkeley sense of the word. I mean that in the academic sense of the word.

286
00:35:58,360 --> 00:36:06,540
In other words, what we'll do is uncover potential that we have inside us, that we've had inside us all along.

287
00:36:06,680 --> 00:36:10,600
Maybe we just didn't see, or maybe it was obscured by something or another.

288
00:36:11,520 --> 00:36:17,100
We'll uncover it so that we can utilize it, so that we can focus on it, so that we can perceive it.

289
00:36:19,580 --> 00:36:23,980
Here's a story just to illustrate this. So this is Michelangelo.

290
00:36:24,820 --> 00:36:35,420
One day he was asked by a journalist of his time, um how did you create this most amazing how did you create this most amazing

291
00:36:35,420 --> 00:36:44,400
masterpiece david to which michelangelo responded it was easy i went to the quarry

292
00:36:44,400 --> 00:36:54,600
i saw this huge piece of marble and in it i saw david all i needed to do was to chip away

293
00:36:54,600 --> 00:36:56,760
the excess stone

294
00:36:57,860 --> 00:37:01,460
to get rid of the marble that shouldn't have been there.

295
00:37:02,360 --> 00:37:04,580
And when I got rid of this excess stone,

296
00:37:05,300 --> 00:37:06,380
there was David.

297
00:37:07,120 --> 00:37:08,080
Now, obviously,

298
00:37:08,660 --> 00:37:14,840
easier said than done, but the story captures the metaphor of what this class very much is about.

299
00:37:15,260 --> 00:37:20,700
It's about chipping away the excess stone. It's about getting rid of limitations, of barriers,

300
00:37:21,240 --> 00:37:31,400
whether it's the fear of failure, something that we didn't have as kids but today most people in our culture have it it's about chipping away perfectionism

301
00:37:31,400 --> 00:37:41,980
that is debilitating and that often hurts us it's about chipping away our ability for success

302
00:37:41,980 --> 00:37:51,540
because maybe we're afraid of success maybe we feel guilty about some of the things that we have in our lives and that in turn limits us

303
00:37:52,280 --> 00:37:57,700
maybe it's about chipping away the limitations in our relationships and why we don't thrive within them.

304
00:37:58,880 --> 00:38:01,440
This is what this class is mostly about.

305
00:38:03,040 --> 00:38:04,360
As Thoreau says,

306
00:38:05,460 --> 00:38:12,080
the soul grows more by subtraction than by addition,

307
00:38:12,980 --> 00:38:22,520
by getting rid of these limitations, limitations that are preventing us from fulfilling our potential because our potential is in there in nature. And we'll talk a lot about human nature.

308
00:38:22,520 --> 00:38:25,400
It's there, whether it's through God, whether it's through evolution.

309
00:38:26,340 --> 00:38:32,740
We have a lot of potential that over time with external voices, with being part of our culture,

310
00:38:33,640 --> 00:38:39,160
that very often these limitations are put on top of us, just like the excess stone.

311
00:38:40,600 --> 00:38:48,240
Lao Tzu, in pursuit of knowledge, every day something is acquired. In pursuit of wisdom, every day something is dropped.

312
00:38:50,430 --> 00:38:52,450
Knowledge is about information.

313
00:38:53,110 --> 00:38:55,470
Wisdom is about transformation.

314
00:39:05,820 --> 00:39:11,920
I was recently interviewed for a newsletter on coaching

315
00:39:12,900 --> 00:39:24,360
before a large conference on the topic. And the interviewer asked me, so what tips, what tools can you give from positive psychology? So I talked about some of the quote-unquote greatest hits,

316
00:39:24,620 --> 00:39:36,360
the importance of gratitude, the importance of physical exercise. I talked about the importance of spending time on our relationships, about taking time aside and simplifying, and so on and so on.

317
00:39:37,500 --> 00:39:48,360
And as I was going through my long list, she stopped me and she said, you know, this is all good, important stuff, but our readers already know that.

318
00:39:48,500 --> 00:39:50,380
I'm looking for the wow factor.

319
00:39:50,960 --> 00:39:53,920
You know, come on, surprise me. What can you tell our readers?

320
00:39:54,920 --> 00:40:02,420
And I thought about this question for a minute, and I realized that there is no wow.

321
00:40:03,500 --> 00:40:08,540
And I told her that. You know, if there is a wow, the wow is that there is no wow.

322
00:40:09,900 --> 00:40:10,640
That's it.

323
00:40:11,000 --> 00:40:20,400
You see, because the overnight transformation emperor, the emperor of quick fixes, has no clothes.

324
00:40:21,100 --> 00:40:24,240
It doesn't exist. It's over-promising and under-delivering.

325
00:40:25,000 --> 00:40:31,640
A good life, a fulfilling life, a rich life includes ups and downs, includes pain and getting up again.

326
00:40:32,040 --> 00:40:36,480
It includes failure and getting up again. It includes success and celebrating it.

327
00:40:37,200 --> 00:40:41,180
Victories and losses, ups and downs, as we'll talk about next week.

328
00:40:42,240 --> 00:40:50,880
It's not about this one secret, one wow to the good life. And many of the things that you will learn in this class, you would have heard of before.

329
00:40:51,560 --> 00:40:53,040
probably nothing new to you.

330
00:40:53,780 --> 00:40:56,200
You already know it inside of you.

331
00:40:59,520 --> 00:41:04,420
And you're going to say, well, this is common sense. And yes, a lot of it is common sense.

332
00:41:05,180 --> 00:41:09,780
However, as Voltaire once said, common sense is not that common.

333
00:41:10,600 --> 00:41:17,980
And this especially applies to application. So the aim of this class is to make common sense more common,

334
00:41:18,540 --> 00:41:20,240
especially in the realm of application.

335
00:41:27,540 --> 00:41:30,000
At the end of the class, here is what I'm hoping for.

336
00:41:30,380 --> 00:41:42,120
At the end of the class, if you decide to take it, the end of the semester, I don't think, and I'm not expecting you to come and tell me, wow, Tal, thank you for teaching me so many new things.

337
00:41:43,760 --> 00:41:46,200
That's not what I'm expecting. I don't think that is what will happen.

338
00:41:46,560 --> 00:41:59,180
What I hope will happen is for you to come and say, rather than thank you for teaching me something new, you would say, thank you for reminding me of something that I've already known.

339
00:41:59,750 --> 00:42:03,200
And this is what the class is about. It's a constant reminder, twice a week.

340
00:42:04,680 --> 00:42:10,500
Constant reminder of what you already know, of what is inside you. The David that is inside you.

341
00:42:10,980 --> 00:42:20,400
And what this class will hopefully do is help you to chip away some of these limitations. Whether it's cognitive limitations that prevent you from seeing what you already knew.

342
00:42:21,760 --> 00:42:31,280
Emotional limitations that are preventing you from deriving the benefits of what you already know. or behavioral limitations.

343
00:42:32,260 --> 00:42:38,480
The ABC, affect, behavior, and cognition that we'll talk about during the change week.

344
00:42:39,220 --> 00:42:41,940
It's about making common sense more common.

345
00:42:42,940 --> 00:42:52,360
Information in and of itself is simply not enough. It's not enough, and what we need in addition to our information highway is a transformation highway.

346
00:42:53,760 --> 00:42:56,320
Transformation highway or transformation back roads.

347
00:42:57,020 --> 00:43:06,020
To counter the fast, increasing pace, because as we'll talk about next time, rates of depression are on the rise, rates of anxiety are on the rise, not just in this country.

348
00:43:06,460 --> 00:43:13,880
Globally, it's literally a global epidemic. And to deal with it, more information will just not do, just not enough.

349
00:43:14,420 --> 00:43:19,540
Here is Archibald MacLeish, who was a poet, was a Harvard professor.

350
00:43:22,100 --> 00:43:29,680
What is wrong is not the great discoveries of science. Information is always better than ignorance, no matter what information or what ignorance.

351
00:43:30,120 --> 00:43:36,820
What is wrong is the belief behind the information, the belief that information will change the world.

352
00:43:37,640 --> 00:43:38,640
It won't.

353
00:43:39,080 --> 00:43:49,020
Just adding and filling up our containers with more and more stuff, more and more information, more and more data, it's just not enough. We need more than that.

354
00:43:49,760 --> 00:43:57,520
This class will take the humanistic approach. And let me read you a quick excerpt by Abraham Maslow, who talks about this approach.

355
00:43:59,120 --> 00:44:09,540
If one took a course or picked up a book on the psychology of learning, most of it, in my opinion, would be beside the point, that is, beside the humanistic point.

356
00:44:09,800 --> 00:44:20,060
Most of it would present learning as the acquisition of associations, of skills and capacities that are external and not intrinsic to the human character,

357
00:44:20,060 --> 00:44:24,140
to the human personality, to the person himself.

358
00:44:26,080 --> 00:44:28,580
External refers to information.

359
00:44:29,500 --> 00:44:38,000
Internal refers to the transformation, the changing of the form. And when we talk about transformation, I actually mean it quite literally,

360
00:44:38,540 --> 00:44:43,980
changing of the form, changing of the brain. As we'll talk about when we talk about meditation, for instance,

361
00:44:44,900 --> 00:44:48,700
our brain today can actually, we know that our brain today can actually change.

362
00:44:50,060 --> 00:44:58,900
And through fMRI studies, since 1998, a new concept came up, which is neurogenesis or neuroplasticity,

363
00:44:59,340 --> 00:45:10,420
meaning our brain actually changes, transforms, changes its form throughout our life. So I don't just mean it metaphorically. I also very often mean it literally.

364
00:45:11,700 --> 00:45:17,960
Abraham Maslow continues, Humanistic philosophy offers a new conception of learning, of teaching, and of education.

365
00:45:18,620 --> 00:45:28,840
Stated simply, such a concept holds that the function of education, the goal of education, the human goal, the humanistic goal, the goal so far as human beings are concerned,

366
00:45:29,060 --> 00:45:40,560
is ultimately the self-actualization of a person, the becoming fully human, the development of the fullest height that the human species can stand up to or that the particular individual can come to.

367
00:45:41,360 --> 00:45:47,320
In a less technical way, it is helping the person to become the best that he is able to become.

368
00:45:49,400 --> 00:45:53,780
And this was before the ad came out for the army. I'd be all you can be.

369
00:45:54,720 --> 00:45:56,520
This is what the class is about.

370
00:45:57,480 --> 00:46:02,760
It's the humanistic approach. It's about fulfilling our potential, chipping away those limitations.

371
00:46:04,200 --> 00:46:08,380
Now, to many of you, this may sound naive, idealistic.

372
00:46:09,700 --> 00:46:16,680
Naive it is not. Idealistic it is. And we'll talk about and discuss the importance of idealism and maintaining idealism

373
00:46:16,680 --> 00:46:24,640
if we are to introduce personal change, interpersonal change, or community or society change.

374
00:46:27,140 --> 00:46:36,740
This class is not about providing answers concerning the good life or happiness. It is about identifying the right questions.

375
00:46:40,960 --> 00:46:47,720
Ask and you shall receive, say the scripture. This class is what I think education is all about,

376
00:46:47,720 --> 00:46:59,620
which is the quest for information and transformation, must begin with a question. Quest, question. It's no coincidence that there is an etymological link between the two.

377
00:47:00,520 --> 00:47:12,060
In this class, you will be asking, you will be asked many questions. Questions that, as you will see, create reality. We'll talk about that already next time. The importance of the question you ask of yourselves, first and foremost,

378
00:47:12,300 --> 00:47:17,280
of your partner, of your students, of your parents, of your employees in the future,

379
00:47:17,820 --> 00:47:19,340
teammates, and so on.

380
00:47:19,880 --> 00:47:21,400
Questions make a difference.

381
00:47:22,860 --> 00:47:30,320
Peter Drucker, the most common source of mistakes in management decisions is the emphasis on finding the right answer rather than the right question.

382
00:47:30,500 --> 00:47:42,040
Peter Drucker, considered the most important management scholar of the 20th century, just recently passed away, saying that the biggest mistake is not asking the right questions.

